      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we can use the fundamental principle of counting, which states that if there are \\( n \\) ways to do one thing and \\( m \\) ways to do another, then there are \\( n \\times m \\) ways to do both. We can apply this principle to each of the three categories (entrees, drinks, and desserts) to find the total number of distinct possible meals Crystal can buy.\n\n1. **Entrees:**\n   Crystal has 4 entrees to choose from:\n   - Pizza\n   - Chicken Teriyaki\n   - Corn Dog\n   - Fish and Chips\n\n   So, there are 4 ways to choose an entree.\n\n2. **Drinks:**\n   Crystal has 2 drinks to choose from:\n   - Lemonade\n   - Root Beer\n\n   So, there are 2 ways to choose a drink.\n\n3. **Desserts:**\n   Crystal has 2 desserts to choose from:\n   - Frozen Yogurt\n   - Chocolate Chip Cookie\n\n   So, there are 2 ways to choose a dessert.\n\nUsing the fundamental principle of counting, the total number of distinct possible meals is calculated as follows:\n\\[\n4 \\text{ (entrees)} \\times 2 \\text{ (drinks)} \\times 2 \\text{ (desserts)} = 4 \\times 2 \\times 2 = 16\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct possible meals Crystal can buy is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).",
